some compounds smell really good because  of the chemicals they contain cinnamon  and vanilla have a pleasant odor that  you might call aromatic when an organic  chemist uses this word they're likely  referring to aromaticity which is a  special feature of some compounds now I  don't want to imply that all molecules  with aromaticity smell good pyridine is  an aromatic compound with an unpleasant  fish-like odor and this aromatic  pyridine salt has no sense at all  additionally compounds that are not  aromatic can have strong odors so if  aromaticity isn't tied to smell how can  we tell if a compound is aromatic let's  begin by talking about benzene the  classical example of an aromatic  compound we can draw a resonance form  for benzene where it looks like the  double bonds have moved but this isn't  the whole story at all there's a  continuous system of P orbitals above  and below the ring the electrons are  actually delocalized around the ring  circling around the PI system an  electron density map of benzene shows  red hot spots of electron density where  the PI electrons are concentrated  although some benzene representations  seem to have alternating single and  double bonds each bond is 1.3 9  angstroms long which is between the  length of a carbon-carbon double and  single bond this is due to the  delocalization of electrons that we  discussed and it makes aromatic  compounds rather stable to be aromatic a  cyclic compound must have a continuous  PI system and be planar it must also  satisfy huckel's rule having 4n plus 2  electrons in the PI system where n is 0  or a small positive number  now compounds that contain 4n electrons  in the PI system are particularly  unstable and are referred to as anti  aromatic we need to be able to identify  aromatic compounds because they have  unique reactivity when an alkene is  treated with bromine the double bond  acts as a nucleophile forming a cation  that is attacked by BR - the end result  is the addition of BR 2 a